How can we implement a binary search algorithm in Go? Can you provide an example Go code that illustrates how binary search works?
[Go code]:
package main
import "fmt"
func binarySearch(arr []int, left int, right int, x int) int {
    for left <= right {
        mid := left + (right-left)/2
        if arr[mid] == x {
            return mid
        } else if arr[mid] < x {
            left = mid + 1
        } else {
            right = mid - 1
        }
    }
    return -1
}
func main() {
    arr := []int{2, 5, 7, 10, 14, 17, 20}
    n := len(arr)
    x := 14
    result := binarySearch(arr, 0, n-1, x)
    if result == -1 {
        fmt.Println("Element not present")
    } else {
        fmt.Println("Element found at index", result)
    }
}
How can we modify this Go code to handle arrays with duplicate elements?

output:
To modify the Go code to handle arrays with duplicate elements, we can make a small change in the condition for checking if the middle element is equal to the target element. Instead of returning immediately if they are equal, we can continue the search to the left and right of the middle element to find all occurrences of the target element. Here's the modified Go code:
```
package main
import "fmt"
func binarySearch(arr []int, left int, right int, x int) []int {
    var result []int
    for left <= right {
        mid := left + (right-left)/2
        if arr[mid] == x {
            result = append(result, mid)
            // search left for duplicates
            i := mid - 1
            for i >= left && arr[i] == x {
                result = append(result, i)
                i--
            }
            // search right for duplicates
            j := mid + 1
            for j <= right && arr[j] == x {
                result = append(result, j)
                j++
            }
            return result
        } else if arr[mid] < x {
            left = mid + 1
        } else {
            right = mid - 1
        }
    }
    return result
}
func main() {
    arr := []int{2, 5, 7, 10, 14, 14, 14, 17, 20}
    n := len(arr)
    x := 14
    result := binarySearch(arr, 0, n-1, x)
    if len(result) == 0 {
        fmt.Println("Element not present")
    } else {
        fmt.Println("Element found at indices", result)
    }
}
```
In this modified code, we use a slice to store the indices of all occurrences of the target element. After finding the first occurrence of the target element, we search to the left and right of the middle element to find all duplicates. We continue the search until we reach the left or right end of the array, or until we find an element that is not equal to the target element. Finally, we return the slice containing all the indices of the target element.

