To simulate lunar phases during a full moon night atop a mountain on your website using JavaScript, you can use the following steps:
1. Create a canvas element on your HTML page where you want to display the lunar phases.
2. Use JavaScript to draw the moon on the canvas element. You can use the `arc()` method to draw a circle for the moon and fill it with a gray color.
3. To create the phases of the moon, you can use the `globalCompositeOperation` property of the canvas element. This property allows you to combine the colors of two overlapping shapes.
4. For the full moon, keep the moon circle as it is. For other phases, you can create a smaller circle and position it over the moon circle. Then, set the `globalCompositeOperation` property to `destination-out` to remove the overlapping portion of the smaller circle, creating the effect of a crescent moon.
Here is an example HTML code that you can use to achieve this effect:
```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<title>Lunar Phases</title>
</head>
<body>
	<canvas id="moonCanvas"></canvas>
	<script>
		var canvas = document.getElementById("moonCanvas");
		var context = canvas.getContext("2d");
		// Set canvas size to the window size
		canvas.width = window.innerWidth;
		canvas.height = window.innerHeight;
		// Draw the moon
		context.beginPath();
		context.arc(canvas.width/2, canvas.height/2, 150, 0, 2*Math.PI);
		context.fillStyle = "#999";
		context.fill();
		// Create a smaller circle for the crescent moon
		context.beginPath();
		context.arc(canvas.width/2 - 50, canvas.height/2, 150, 0, 2*Math.PI);
		context.fillStyle = "#fff";
		context.fill();
		// Set the globalCompositeOperation property to create the crescent moon effect
		context.globalCompositeOperation = "destination-out";
		context.beginPath();
		context.arc(canvas.width/2 + 50, canvas.height/2, 150, 0, 2*Math.PI);
		context.fill();
	</script>
</body>
</html>
```
You can modify the size and position of the circles to create different phases of the moon. Additionally, you can use CSS to position the canvas element on top of a mountain background image to create a realistic celestial view.

C++ can be used for air pollution analysis by creating algorithms that process and analyze data from various sources such as air quality sensors, satellite imagery, and weather data. The algorithms can be designed to identify patterns and trends in the data that may indicate the presence of pollutants and their potential sources.
Effective methods for measuring pollutants include using air quality sensors, which can be stationary or mobile, and satellite imagery, which can detect emissions from industrial facilities and wildfires. Tracking the origins of pollutants can be done by analyzing wind patterns and using atmospheric models to simulate the movement of pollutants.
Here is some sample code for analyzing air pollution data:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;
int main()
{
    // Read data from a file
    ifstream dataFile("air_pollution_data.txt");
    string line;
    vector<double> pollutantLevels;
    while (getline(dataFile, line))
    {
        double level = stod(line);
        pollutantLevels.push_back(level);
    }
    // Calculate average pollutant level
    double sum = 0;
    for (double level : pollutantLevels)
    {
        sum += level;
    }
    double average = sum / pollutantLevels.size();
    // Identify high pollutant levels
    vector<double> highLevels;
    for (double level : pollutantLevels)
    {
        if (level > average * 1.5)
        {
            highLevels.push_back(level);
        }
    }
    // Output results
    cout << "Average pollutant level: " << average << endl;
    cout << "Number of high pollutant levels: " << highLevels.size() << endl;
    cout << "High pollutant levels: ";
    for (double level : highLevels)
    {
        cout << level << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
This code reads data from a file containing pollutant levels, calculates the average level, and identifies high levels that are 50% above the average. This is just a simple example, and more complex algorithms can be developed to analyze data related to weather patterns, industrial emissions, transportation emissions, wildfires, and dust storms.

