import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Random;
public class MagneticBallSimulation {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        // create an array list to hold the magnetic balls
        ArrayList<MagneticBall> magneticBalls = new ArrayList<>();

        // create 10 magnetic balls with random magnet strength and polarity
        for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
            Random rand = new Random();
            int magnetStrength = rand.nextInt(10) + 1;
            String materialComposition = "Iron";
            String magneticPolarity = rand.nextBoolean() ? "North" : "South";
            MagneticBall ball = new MagneticBall(magnetStrength, materialComposition, magneticPolarity);
            magneticBalls.add(ball);
        }

        // simulate the magnetic field and ball movements
        for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < magneticBalls.size(); j++) {
                MagneticBall ball1 = magneticBalls.get(j);
                for (int k = j + 1; k < magneticBalls.size(); k++) {
                    MagneticBall ball2 = magneticBalls.get(k);
                    double distance = Math.sqrt(Math.pow(ball1.getX() - ball2.getX(), 2) + Math.pow(ball1.getY() - ball2.getY(), 2));
                    if (distance <= ball1.getMagnetStrength() && distance <= ball2.getMagnetStrength()) {
                        ball1.attract(ball2);
                        ball2.attract(ball1);
                    } else {
                        ball1.repulse(ball2);
                        ball2.repulse(ball1);
                    }
                }
                ball1.move();
            }
        }

        // print the final positions of the magnetic balls
        for (MagneticBall ball : magneticBalls) {
            System.out.println("Ball " + ball.getId() + " is at (" + ball.getX() + ", " + ball.getY() + ")");
        }
    }
}
```
In this program, we first create an array list to hold the magnetic balls. We then create 10 magnetic balls with random magnet strength and polarity, and add them to the array list.
We then simulate the magnetic field and ball movements by iterating through each ball and checking its distance to every other ball. If the distance is less than or equal to the magnet strength of both balls, we call the `attract` method of each ball to attract them towards each other. Otherwise, we call the `repulse` method of each ball to repel them away from each other. We then call the `move` method of each ball to update its position based on its velocity.
Finally, we print the final positions of the magnetic balls.
Here's the code for the MagneticBall class:
```
import java.util.Random;
public class MagneticBall {

    private static int nextId = 1;

    private int id;
    private int magnetStrength;
    private String materialComposition;
    private String magneticPolarity;
    private double x;
    private double y;
    private double vx;
    private double vy;

    public MagneticBall(int magnetStrength, String materialComposition, String magneticPolarity) {
        this.id = nextId++;
        this.magnetStrength = magnetStrength;
        this.materialComposition = materialComposition;
        this.magneticPolarity = magneticPolarity;
        Random rand = new Random();
        this.x = rand.nextDouble() * 100;
        this.y = rand.nextDouble() * 100;
        this.vx = rand.nextDouble() * 10 - 5;
        this.vy = rand.nextDouble() * 10 - 5;
    }

    public int getId() {
        return id;
    }

    public int getMagnetStrength() {
        return magnetStrength;
    }

    public String getMaterialComposition() {
        return materialComposition;
    }

    public String getMagneticPolarity() {
        return magneticPolarity;
    }

    public double getX() {
        return x;
    }

    public double getY() {
        return y;
    }

    public void attract(MagneticBall ball) {
        double dx = ball.getX() - x;
        double dy = ball.getY() - y;
        double distance = Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
        double force = magnetStrength * ball.getMagnetStrength() / distance;
        double angle = Math.atan2(dy, dx);
        double fx = force * Math.cos(angle);
        double fy = force * Math.sin(angle);
        vx += fx;
        vy += fy;
        ball.setVx(ball.getVx() - fx);
        ball.setVy(ball.getVy() - fy);
    }

    public void repulse(MagneticBall ball) {
        double dx = ball.getX() - x;
        double dy = ball.getY() - y;
        double distance = Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
        double force = magnetStrength * ball.getMagnetStrength() / Math.pow(distance, 2);
        double angle = Math.atan2(dy, dx);
        double fx = -force * Math.cos(angle);
        double fy = -force * Math.sin(angle);
        vx += fx;
        vy += fy;
        ball.setVx(ball.getVx() - fx);
        ball.setVy(ball.getVy() - fy);
    }

    public void move() {
        x += vx;
        y += vy;
        if (x < 0) {
            x = -x;
            vx = -vx;
        } else if (x > 100) {
            x = 200 - x;
            vx = -vx;
        }
        if (y < 0) {
            y = -y;
            vy = -vy;
        } else if (y > 100) {
            y = 200 - y;
            vy = -vy;
        }
    }

    public double getVx() {
        return vx;
    }

    public void setVx(double vx) {
        this.vx = vx;
    }

    public double getVy() {
        return vy;
    }

    public void setVy(double vy) {
        this.vy = vy;
    }
}
```
In this class, we define the attributes and methods of the MagneticBall class. The `attract` method calculates the force of attraction between two magnetic balls based on their magnet strengths and distances, and updates their velocities accordingly. The `repulse` method calculates the force of repulsion between two magnetic balls based on their magnet strengths and distances, and updates their velocities accordingly. The `move` method updates the position of the ball based on its velocity, and ensures that it bounces off the walls of the simulation area.
