Yes, I can definitely help you with that! Here's an interactive HTML page that allows users to input a set of numbers and determine if it's feasible to arrange them into two subsets, where the sum of each subset is equal:
```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<title>Subset Sum Problem</title>
	<script type="text/javascript">
		function checkSubsetSum() {
			var input = document.getElementById("numbers").value;
			var nums = input.split(",");
			var sum = 0;
			for (var i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {
				sum += parseInt(nums[i]);
			}
			if (sum % 2 != 0) {
				document.getElementById("result").innerHTML = "It is not possible to divide these numbers into two subsets with equal sum.";
				return;
			}
			var target = sum / 2;
			var dp = new Array(nums.length + 1);
			for (var i = 0; i < dp.length; i++) {
				dp[i] = new Array(target + 1).fill(false);
			}
			dp[0][0] = true;
			for (var i = 1; i <= nums.length; i++) {
				for (var j = 0; j <= target; j++) {
					dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j];
					if (j >= parseInt(nums[i - 1])) {
						dp[i][j] = dp[i][j] || dp[i - 1][j - parseInt(nums[i - 1])];
					}
				}
			}
			if (dp[nums.length][target]) {
				document.getElementById("result").innerHTML = "It is possible to divide these numbers into two subsets with equal sum.";
			} else {
				document.getElementById("result").innerHTML = "It is not possible to divide these numbers into two subsets with equal sum.";
			}
		}
	</script>
</head>
<body>
	<h1>Subset Sum Problem</h1>
	<p>Enter 10 integers separated by commas:</p>
	<input type="text" id="numbers">
	<button type="button" onclick="checkSubsetSum()">Check</button>
	<p id="result"></p>
</body>
</html>
```
This HTML page uses JavaScript to check if it's possible to divide the input numbers into two subsets with equal sum. If it's possible, it will display a message saying so. If it's not possible, it will display a message saying so.
The algorithm used in this HTML page is the dynamic programming solution to the subset sum problem. It optimizes the arrangement of numbers, resulting in faster and more accurate calculations. The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n * target), where n is the number of input numbers and target is the sum of all input numbers divided by 2.
