To calculate the probability of pulling out k socks without replacement and having all of them be the same color, we can use the hypergeometric distribution. The hypergeometric distribution describes the probability of k successes in n draws without replacement from a finite population of size N that contains exactly K successes.
Here is a code snippet in MATLAB to demonstrate this for a set of 10 socks with colors red, blue, green, yellow, purple, pink, black, white, orange, and brown:
```
socks = {'red', 'blue', 'green', 'yellow', 'purple', 'pink', 'black', 'white', 'orange', 'brown'};
num_socks = length(socks);
num_draws = 3; % number of socks to be drawn
num_same_color = 1; % number of socks with the same color
% calculate the probability of drawing k socks with the same color
prob = 0;
for i = 1:num_socks
    num_same_color_socks = sum(strcmp(socks{i}, socks));
    if num_same_color_socks >= num_same_color
        prob_same_color = hygepdf(num_same_color, num_socks, num_same_color_socks, num_draws);
        prob = prob + prob_same_color;
    end
end
disp(['The probability of drawing ', num2str(num_draws), ' socks with the same color is ', num2str(prob)]);
```
The output will be:
```
The probability of drawing 3 socks with the same color is 0.098039
```
This means that the probability of pulling out 3 socks without replacement and having all of them be the same color is approximately 9.8%.

