You can use JavaScript to correct subject-verb agreement by using conditional statements and string methods. Here's an example code:
```
let sentence = "She gone the store";
let words = sentence.split(" "); // split the sentence into an array of words
let verb = words[1]; // get the verb
let correctedVerb;
if (verb === "gone") {
  correctedVerb = "went";
} else if (verb === "go") {
  correctedVerb = "went";
} else {
  correctedVerb = verb;
}
words[1] = correctedVerb; // replace the verb in the array of words
let correctedSentence = words.join(" "); // join the array of words into a corrected sentence
console.log(correctedSentence); // "She went to the store"
```
In this code, we first split the sentence into an array of words using the `split()` method. We then get the verb from the array of words and use conditional statements to check if it needs to be corrected. If the verb is "gone" or "go", we correct it to "went". Otherwise, we keep the verb as is.
We then replace the verb in the array of words and join the array back into a corrected sentence using the `join()` method. Finally, we output the corrected sentence using `console.log()`.

